This programs implements the Split Variational Autoencoder (SplitVAE) for disentangling structured representations from trajectory data in linear time-invariant (LTI) systems.
The model learns from both noiseless(model) and noisy(observed) trajectories.
SplitVAE separates the latent space into two parts: z₁ capturing only noisy features and z₂ capturing common features. 
The loss function combines mean squared reconstruction error, KL divergences for both latent components, and a noise-aware regularization term. 
After training for 1000 epochs on 500 noisy and 1000 noiseless trajectories, the decoder is used to generate 1000 synthetic trajectories by sampling from the disentangled latent spaces.
This setup enables interpretable and noise-resilient trajectory generation for downstream tasks like control, simulation, or system identification.
